      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we can use modular arithmetic. Let the four positive integers be \\(a\\), \\(b\\), \\(c\\), and \\(d\\). According to the problem, we have the following congruences:\n\n\\[a \\equiv 2 \\pmod{11}\\]\n\\[b \\equiv 4 \\pmod{11}\\]\n\\[c \\equiv 6 \\pmod{11}\\]\n\\[d \\equiv 8 \\pmod{11}\\]\n\nWe need to find the remainder when the sum \\(a + b + c + d\\) is divided by 11. Using the properties of congruences, we can add these congruences together:\n\n\\[\na + b + c + d \\equiv 2 + 4 + 6 + 8 \\pmod{11}\n\\]\n\nNow, let's calculate the sum on the right-hand side:\n\n\\[\n2 + 4 + 6 + 8 = 20\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the remainder when 20 is divided by 11. This can be done by performing the division:\n\n\\[\n20 \\div 11 = 1 \\text{ remainder } 9\n\\]\n\nSo, the remainder when 20 is divided by 11 is 9. Therefore, the remainder when the sum of the four integers is divided by 11 is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{9}\n\\]",
